<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stele of the Vultures</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stele of the Vultures is a fragmentary limestone monument erected around 2450 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, to celebrate his victory over the neighbouring city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>. It shows the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a> holding Lagash's enemies in a net and Eannatum leading a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> of soldiers, while vultures carry off the dead. Found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> (Tello), it is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> and is one of the earliest known war monuments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures.jpg" alt="Stele of the Vultures" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Background: Kikuyu3 Elements: Eric Gaba (Sting) Composite: पाटलिपुत्र 10:52, 30 April 2020 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_the_Vultures_in_the_Louvre_Museum_(enhanced_composite).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sumerian warfare had relied heavily on close-order infantry, as depicted on the Standard of Ur and the <b>Stele of the Vultures</b>; Sargon's army appears to have made greater use of lightly equipped troops and archers, which allowed more flexible tactics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Stele of the Vultures</b> shows Eannatum, inspired by Ningirsu, dismount from his chariot and lead his men on foot; with spears lowered the army of Lagash advanced in a dense phalanx and after a brief clash won, one of the earliest organised battles known.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Stele of the Vultures</b>, raised about 2450 BCE by Eannatum of Lagash, celebrates his victory over neighbouring Umma in a long dispute over the land between them, and around 2350 BCE Urukagina of Lagash proclaimed reforms against the exactions of officials and priests.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The war with Lagash is told from the Lagash side, notably on Eannatum's <b>Stele of the Vultures</b> and in the inscriptions of Enmetena, which describe Umma breaking treaties and seizing land.</blockquote><a href="/state/umma">Umma</a></li></ul></section>
